# Backend modularity (`app/`)

Legacy framework patterns (views holding business logic, `ensure_*_table`
boot-DDL helpers, thread-pool workers) are **not** templates for the new code.

## Placement

| Kind of change | Put it in |
|----------------|-----------|
| HTTP/WS/job/internal handler | `*/adapters/` — map protocol ↔ domain, nothing else |
| Business mutation / query | `*/domain/<feature>/` — one async function per mutation |
| ORM mapping | `*/models/` — thin; no permission math |
| DB/cache/storage/realtime | `*/infra/` |
| Shared primitive (id, time, hash, errors) | `platform/core/` — **search this first** |
| Staff capability | `platform/domain/authz` |
| Tenant capability | `app/domain/authz` |
| Cross-tenant social features | `social/` — not `app` tables |
| Users / sessions / MFA | `identity/` |

Forbidden: a context importing another context's tables; adapters importing
other adapters' private helpers; framework request types in domain; a second
copy of an existing use-case.

## Shape of a use-case

```text
open UoW → load resource (IDOR: tenant id matches)
  → access.authorize(actor, action, resource)
  → mutate
  → commit
  → outbox event (same transaction)
```

HTTP and WS both call this. If you copied gates into a second handler, stop
and extract.

## Size

New modules stay small. If a file is heading toward a 3k-line god-module,
split by use-case **before** adding more. Vertical slice > "I'll organize
later."

## Registry

Mutating use-cases must appear in the action→permission registry.
Unregistered mutate = fail. Until a linter exists, add the mapping next to
the use-case in the same change.
